  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If your roof insurance claim was denied, do not assume the decision is final, as many denials can be successfully challenged or reversed with proper documentation.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          ", as many denials can be successfully challenged or reversed with proper documentation"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "1. Read the Denial Letter Closely"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Identify the exact reason: Insurers typically deny claims due to \"normal wear and tear,\" \"pre-existing damage,\" \"cosmetic damage only,\" or \"insufficient documentation\".",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              3,
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Review your policy: Check your declarations page and specific policy exclusions to see how the stated reason aligns with your coverage.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              5
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "2. Get an Independent Roof Inspection"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Hire a licensed, reputable local roofing contractor to conduct a thorough, independent inspection.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              4,
              5
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Ask them to document all slopes (including ones the original insurance adjuster may have skipped), take clear photos/videos, and write a technical scope of work that specifically ties the damage to a storm or covered event.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              4,
              6
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Gather third-party storm verification, such as a National Weather Service (NWS) report or certified hail/wind report for the date of the storm.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              6,
              7
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "3. Request a Formal Reinspection"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Submit a written request to your insurance company for a reinspection, asking to have a different adjuster or a senior field adjuster sent out.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              4,
              8,
              9
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Have your roofer present during the reinspection so they can point out the missed damage and share their itemized findings face-to-face.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              4,
              7
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "4. File a Point-by-Point Appeal or Supplement"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "If the reinspection is rejected, submit a formal appeal letter or supplement package including:",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Your contractor's written report and side-by-side scope comparison ."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "High-resolution photos and date-stamped evidence (or historical satellite/Google Earth imagery if proving pre-storm condition)."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Local weather data confirming the storm event.",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  0,
                  2,
                  3,
                  4,
                  6
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              2,
              3,
              4,
              6
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "5. Escalate if Necessary"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Invoke the Appraisal Clause: If you and the insurer agree that damage exists but disagree heavily on the repair cost or value, check your policy for an appraisal clause to hire independent appraisers.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              4,
              10
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Hire a Public Adjuster: Consider bringing in a licensed public adjuster who works on your behalf (typically for a 10\u201315% fee) to manage complex or stubborn claims. Note that consensus on online forums like notes that public adjusters sometimes promise favorable outcomes to win contracts even on difficult claims.",
            "snippet_links": [
              {
                "link": "https://www.reddit.com/r/Insurance/comments/1busr46/insurance_denied_my_roof_claim_but_it_makes_zero/"
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              4,
              11
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "File a State Insurance Department Complaint: If you believe the insurer is acting in bad faith or ignoring valid proof, file a formal complaint with your state's department of insurance.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              4
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you'd like to narrow down your next step, tell me:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "What specific reason was written on your denial letter?"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Have you already had an independent roofer look at the damage?"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
